\begin_layout Subsection
SWTImageJ in Bio7
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:SWTImageJ"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bio7 embeds 
\series bold
SWTImageJ
\series default
, a version of ImageJ with a native SWT user interface (instead of the former AWT version which was embedded with the SWT_AWT bridge). The images, the menus, the toolbar, the dialogs and the tools of ImageJ are SWT widgets, so they follow the theme of Bio7 (e.g., the dark theme) and work well on high DPI displays. For a description of ImageJ see https://imagej.net/ij/ and the user guide https://imagej.net/ij/docs/guide/. The differences to the regular version of ImageJ are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The images are displayed in the tabs of the 
\series bold
ImageJ-Canvas
\series default
 view or as 
\series bold
detached image views
\series default
 (see below). An image becomes active when its tab or view is selected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The menu of ImageJ (File, Edit, Image, Process, Analyze, Plugins, Window, Help) is the menu of the ImageJ-Canvas view.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The toolbar of ImageJ (with the status line and the progress bar) is the 
\series bold
ImageJ-Toolbar
\series default
 view.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Two actions compile or interpret the source of the active editor on the classpath of ImageJ: 
\series bold
Plugins > Compile Java (Eclipse)
\series default
 and 
\series bold
Plugins > Interpret Script (Eclipse)
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The actions for the transfer of image data to and from R are available in the 
\series bold
Image-Methods
\series default
 view (
\series bold
Window > Bio7-Toolbar
\series default
, see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:R-ImageJ-Analysis-Perspective"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Installed plugins extend the menus of the ImageJ-Canvas view. Macros can extend the main menu of Bio7 and can be created like in the regular version.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Images, folders (as a stack) and plugins can be dropped on the ImageJ-Canvas view.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The ImageJ API is accessible with the embedded Java compiler and with BeanShell, Groovy, JavaScript, ImageJ Macro or Jython scripts. Many examples of the ImageJ website can be executed directly in the editors of Bio7.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Image Views and the Window Menu
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An image can be displayed in its own view: 
\series bold
Window > Open Current Image as View
\series default
 detaches the active image, 
\series bold
Window > Open All Images As Views
\series default
 detaches all images and with 
\series bold
Window > Open Images As Views By Default
\series default
 new images are opened as views (also a preference). By default a detached view is opened in its own window which follows the active perspective (see the ImageJ preferences). Closing the view closes the image. Many images (e.g., the images of a stack, Image > Stacks > Stack to Images) are opened as views quickly in one step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Window > Cascade Image Views
\series default
 and 
\series bold
Window > Tile Image Views
\series default
 arrange the windows of the image views: cascaded (with the sizes of the windows) or as a grid which fills the monitor. With more than one monitor a submenu selects the monitor. 
\series bold
Window > Fullscreen Image (F2)
\series default
 displays the active image in the fullscreen mode (F2 the primary monitor, F3 the secondary, F4 the tertiary, F5 a selected monitor, Escape exits).
\end_layout

